eaebc8c5-533d-4957-9a27-b2e9ca18583e	wrong package	Uploaded file: Assignment 2 ci.pdf	file	\N	https://tlczogmizfbjtrbnsrxc.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reports/2026-05-11/c8dd33f2-82bb-4fb3-b18f-e2209f8576c9-Assignment_2_ci.pdf	Assignment 2 ci.pdf	application/pdf	2026-05-11 08:10:34.723333+00	\N	df47d5f4-9178-4c9b-8b37-c1a186e752b1	Assignment 2: Iris Dataset Classification using MLP\r\n\r\nIntroduction:- This assignment is about classifying the Iris dataset using a Multi-Layer\r\nPerceptron model. The Iris dataset contains flower measurements, and the aim is to predict the\r\ncorrect flower class.\r\n\r\nCell 1: Dataset loading\r\n\r\nThe Iris dataset was loaded using Python. The dataset contains 150 samples, 4 input features,\r\nand 3 flower classes.\r\nCell 2: Train/test split and scaling\r\n\r\nThe dataset was divided into training and testing data. 80% of the data was used for training\r\nand 20% was used for testing. The features were scaled using StandardScaler to improve MLP\r\nperformance.Cell 3: Parameter testing using MLP\r\n\r\nDifferent MLP parameter values were tested using GridSearchCV. The parameters tested\r\nincluded hidden layer size, activation function, solver, alpha value, and learning rate.Cell 4: Best parameters and accuracy result\r\n\r\nAfter testing different parameter combinations, the best MLP model was selected based on\r\naccuracy. The best parameters and test accuracy are shown below.Cell 5: Confusion matrix\r\n\r\nThe confusion matrix shows how many flower samples were correctly and incorrectly classified\r\nby the model.\r\n\r\nConclusion:- The MLP model successfully classified the Iris dataset. By testing different\r\nparameter values, the best model was selected. The result shows that MLP can perform well for\r\nIris flower classification.	{"title":"Assignment 2: Iris Dataset Classification using MLP","summary_steps":"This is an assignment about classifying the Iris dataset using a Multi-Layer Perceptron model.","key_points":"• The assignment involves classifying the Iris dataset using a Multi-Layer Perceptron model.\\n• The Iris dataset contains 150 samples, 4 input features, and 3 flower classes.\\n• Data was split into 80% training and 20% testing, with features scaled using StandardScaler.\\n• GridSearchCV was used to test MLP parameters including hidden layer size and activation function.\\n• The best MLP model was selected based on accuracy, and a confusion matrix was generated.","sop":"Document Type / Title\\n• Assignment 2: Iris Dataset Classification using MLP\\n\\nImportant Concepts / Topics\\n• Iris dataset classification\\n• Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) model\\n• Flower measurements and prediction\\n• Dataset loading and splitting\\n• Feature scaling using StandardScaler\\n• Parameter testing using GridSearchCV\\n• Hidden layer size, activation function, solver, alpha, learning rate\\n• Confusion matrix\\n\\nRequirements / Objectives\\n• Classify the Iris dataset using an MLP model\\n• Predict the correct flower class\\n• Test different MLP parameter values\\n• Select the best MLP model based on accuracy\\n• Generate a confusion matrix","v":5}	processed	2026-05-11 08:11:30.861+00	\N	\N	other	1	The report describes a machine learning assignment, not a logistics issue, requiring no action.	2026-05-11 08:11:32.117+00	[0.01491437, -0.017566187, 0.018318886, -0.045820545, -0.0070873583, 0.020148892, 0.016311923, -0.010875708, 0.0025385178, -0.010545386, 0.00802155, -0.0011429277, 0.023157088, 0.021864882, 0.10898676, -0.000071474395, -0.007877005, -0.017475242, 0.0018922655, -0.0062913257, -0.0033967656, 0.012556598, 0.018350173, -0.016081026, 0.028462933, -0.03653746, -0.008600639, -0.009170541, 0.03600334, 0.011580665, -0.012270492, -0.0067344247, 0.0059595765, -0.010368477, 0.035924587, 0.039259937, 0.0047543193, -0.0017021325, 0.014884674, -0.012523688, -0.02324911, 0.020136615, -0.0025368195, -0.010098721, -0.010465617, 0.0021329315, 0.020613223, 0.0046836543, -0.020760557